  "response": "J Harlen Bretz was a University of Chicago geologist who mapped the Channeled Scablands during the 1920s, and that long campaign of field mapping was finished well before he first laid his reading of the landscape before his colleagues. Bretz presented his flood hypothesis to a hostile audience at a January 1927 meeting of the Geological Society of Washington. His account of the water's origin was explicit: he attributed the floodwater to meltwater released by a volcanic eruption beneath the Cordilleran Ice Sheet. By contrast, Bretz's critics argued that the scablands were cut by ordinary river erosion acting over millions of years, holding that nothing in the region required a departure from the slow processes visible in modern valleys.\n\nThe Purcell Trench lobe of the Cordilleran Ice Sheet dammed the Clark Fork River to impound Glacial Lake Missoula, and the catastrophic failure of that ice barrier is what produced the colossal discharge now reconstructed for the scabland channels. Only well after that hostile Washington meeting did Joseph Thomas Pardee identify Glacial Lake Missoula in western Montana as the source of the floodwater. Giant current ripples up to about 15 metres high occur on the floor of the Camas Prairie basin in Montana; bedforms of that stature are built only beneath deep and swiftly moving water, so they cannot be squared with the view that ordinary rivers wore these channels down over millions of years.\n\nPardee published \"Unusual Currents in Glacial Lake Missoula, Montana\" in 1942. Because peak flood discharge through the scablands reached an estimated 17 million cubic metres per second, the water tore out Dry Falls in the Grand Coulee, an abandoned cataract roughly 5.6 kilometres wide with a drop of about 120 metres. What anchors a figure of that order is the height to which the floods carried and abandoned their coarsest cargo: the elevation of such debris far above the channel floors is direct evidence that water of the reconstructed volume once filled these valleys to their brims. Ice-rafted granitic erratics rest hundreds of metres above the floors of scabland valleys. The bedrock itself tells its own story: the scabland channels are carved into granite of the Idaho Batholith.\n\nEither the Channeled Scablands record only a single flood event, or they record more than one; one of the two must be the case, and both cannot be. The adjudication therefore turned, in the end, not on a preference for sudden over slow causes but on landforms\u2014the ripple fields, the abandoned cataracts, the stranded boulders\u2014whose dimensions only one class of process can produce.",
